# Hospital sites and the Cemetery Hill / National Cemetery landscape: passages from several reports (see the source on each block).
# OCR letter errors in the 1988 Boundary Study are corrected explicitly with {{saved|fixed}}; fi ligatures in the Record of Treatment are repaired as in 50-clr-rot-v1.xdef.

@ NPS-CLR-ROT-V1
%basis the report's endnotes (park research, damage claims, regimental accounts)
35 | hospitals | Field hospitals sited near water: Union by Rock Creek, Confederates by Willoughby Run or Marsh Creek | H-06,H-05 | 1863-07 | stated | Both armies set field hospitals near potable water, the Union by Rock Creek and the Confederates by Willoughby Run or Marsh Creek | =Both armies established field hospitals near potable water sources, the Union by Rock Creek and the Confederates by Willoughby’s Run or Marsh Creek.
218 | hospitals | Frey property: 19th Massachusetts surgeon's field hospital in the orchard, July 2 | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | On the morning of July 2 a surgeon of the 19th Massachusetts (Hall's Brigade) set up a field hospital on the Frey property and was using the orchard as a hospital when the Union 3rd Corps line broke that evening | =On the morning of July 2, a surgeon of the 19th Massachusetts regiment in Hall’s Brigade established a field hospital on the Frey property. Near the end of the day, Confederate advances approached perilously close to the hospital and when the Union 3rd Corps line broke, the surgeon reported “men, horses, guns, and caissons rushed through the orchard, which we were using for hospital….
272 | hospitals | Leister house: Meade's headquarters July 2-3, hospital after July 3 | bld-leister-house,H-16 | 1863-07 | stated | The Leister house was Meade's headquarters on July 2 and 3 and was used as a hospital after the fighting ceased on July 3 | =The Leister House served as the headquarters for Union General George Meade on July 2 and 3 and was used as a hospital after fighting ceased on July 3.
327 | hospitals | George Spangler farm: field hospital | bld-spangler-farm,H-06 | 1863-07 | stated | In the southern section the George Spangler farm served as a field hospital | =In the southern section, the George Spangler farm served as a field hospital.
329 | hospitals | Lightner farm: Union field hospital and 12th Corps headquarters, July 2-5 | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Lightner farm complex served as a Union field hospital and headquarters of the 12th Corps commander Gen. Williams from July 2 until the morning of July 5 | =Additionally, the farm complex served as a Union field hospital and headquarters for the 12th Corps commander, General Alpheus S. Williams, from July 2 until the morning of July 5. | #s4
333 | hospitals | George Spangler farm complex: cover for Union reserves, 11th Corps field hospital | bld-spangler-farm,H-06 | 1863-07 | stated | The Spangler farm complex gave cover to 5th Corps reserve units and the Artillery Reserve and served as the 11th Corps field hospital; the 1863 period plan shows a stone and rider fence on the east boundary of Field 8 | =The complex provided cover and concealment for Union reserve units of the 5th Corps and the Artillery Reserve and additionally served as the 11th Corps field hospital.9 The park’s 1863 period plan shows the east boundary for Field 8 marked with a stone and rider fence. | #s3
389 | hospitals | Sickles taken to a field hospital along Taneytown Road | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | Sickles, struck in the right leg, was taken to a field hospital along Taneytown Road | =During the fighting, shrapnel struck Sickles in the right leg and he was taken to a field hospital along Taneytown Road.

@ NPS-BOUNDARY-1988
%basis -
17 | hospitals | Wounded treated at hospitals in buildings, farmhouses and barns | - | 1863-07 | stated | The vast numbers of wounded on both sides were treated at hospitals quickly set up in the buildings, farmhouses and barns of Gettysburg | =The vast numbers of wounded on both sides were treated at hospitals quickly established in the buildings, farmhouses and {{baTns|barns}} of Gettysburg.
36 | hospitals | Josiah Benner house and barn used as a hospital | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Josiah Benner house and barn were used as a hospital during the battle | =The Josiah Benner house and barn, still {{standi'nQ|standing}}, were used as a hospital {{duri'n{I|during}} the {{batUe|battle}}.
39 | hospitals | George Spangler farm: Union hospital site and artillery park; 11th Corps hospital where Armistead died | bld-spangler-farm,H-06 | 1863-07 | stated | The 1988 study says the 85-acre George Spangler farm was used by Union forces as a hospital site, artillery park and troop movement area, and that the 11th Corps hospital there was the site of General Armistead's death | =GEORGE SPANGLER FARM (85 ACRES) Used by Union forces as a hospital site, artillery park and troop movement area, this {{Jami|farm}} still exists today. The 11th Corps hospital located here was the site of General Armistead’s death
40 | hospitals | Hospital Woods: remnant of the Camp Letterman general hospital site | - | 1863-07 | stated | The 11 acres of Hospital Woods are all that remain of the site of Camp Letterman, the US General Hospital for about 5,000 Union and Confederate wounded | =HOSPITAL WOODS (11 ACRES) These historic woods are all that rem.ain of the site of Camp Letterman, the U.S. General {{Ho.~pital|Hospital}} for about 5,000 Union and Confederate troops wounded at Gettysburg.
40 | hospitals | Rummel barn: Confederate hospital site | bld-rummel-farm | 1863-07 | stated | Among the historic farm buildings that remain is the Rummel barn, a Confederate hospital site | =Some historic farm buildings remain, including the Rummel barn, a {{Con.federate|Confederate}} hospital site
62 | hospitals | Seminary Old Dorm: observation post, signal station and hospital | H-03,bld-schmucker-hall | 1863-07 | stated | The Old Dorm of the Lutheran Seminary served during the battle as an observation post, signal station and hospital | #s12 | #s9
62 | hospitals | Jacob Weikert farm: field hospitals for Little Round Top and Wheatfield Road casualties | bld-weikert-farm,H-08 | 1863-07 | stated | The two large pre-Civil War buildings of the Jacob Weikert farm were used as field hospitals during and after the battle for the casualties at Little Round Top and the Wheatfield Road | #s14-15
63 | hospitals | Black Horse Tavern (McClellan homestead): large Confederate hospital | - | 1863-07 | stated | The buildings of the Black Horse Tavern complex were used as a large Confederate hospital during and after the battle | #s1-2

@ NPS-CARE-WOUNDED-GUIDE
16 | hospitals | 27,000 wounded after the battle; stretcher bearers collected them each evening | - | 1863-07 | stated | The student guide says there were 27,000 wounded men after the battle and that stretcher bearers and ambulances collected the wounded each evening | =Battle of Gettysburg July 1-3, 1863 There were 27,000 wounded men after the battle. Each evening after that day's battle, stretcher bearers and ambulances collected the wounded.
16 | hospitals | Camp Letterman general hospital open over four months | - | 1863-07 | stated | The student guide says Camp Letterman, a general hospital for the wounded who could not yet travel, was created and did not close for over four months | =Camp Letterman, a general hospital for the wounded who could not yet travel, was created, and did not close for over four months.

@ NPS-COMMON-PRIDE-2
184 | hospitals | 11th Corps 2nd Division hospital at the George Spangler farm (Armistead) | bld-spangler-farm,H-06 | 1863-07-03 | stated | Dr. D. G. Brinton was in charge of the 2nd Division, 11th Corps Union hospital at the George Spangler farm, where General Armistead was brought to die | =Dr. D. G. Brinton, in charge of the 2nd Division, 11th Corps Union hospital at the George Spangler farm, was the attending physician to the Virginia general. | #s1

@ NPS-CLI-CEMETERY
24 | fields | Fence-enclosed fields beside Evergreen Cemetery in 1863 | - | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 the site of the future national cemetery was fence-enclosed fields bordering Evergreen Cemetery, the town's rural cemetery established in 1854 | #s2
24 | general | National cemetery site and Evergreen Cemetery were part of the Union stronghold in the battle | - | 1863-07 | stated | During the battle the national cemetery site and Evergreen Cemetery were part of the Union stronghold along Cemetery Ridge | #s3
16 | earthworks | Evergreen Cemetery: Union batteries on July 2 straddling the boundary line | E-04,E-12 | 1863-07-02 | stated | Evergreen Cemetery was the site of military action on July 2, 1863 and the later boundary between the two cemeteries straddled several Union batteries | Evergreen Cemetery was also the site of military action on July 2, 1863 >>> straddled several Union batteries
28 | woodlots | Pre-Civil War grove in Evergreen Cemetery at the south end of the national cemetery | - | pre-1863 grove (cleared 1956) | stated | While some of the trees in the national cemetery were native, all were managed; the only natural vegetation was a pre-Civil War grove of trees in Evergreen Cemetery that bordered the south end of the national cemetery | While some of the trees in the cemetery were native, all were managed >>> south end of the national cemetery.
28 | buildings | Signal station stood on the high point at the south end of the cemetery during the battle | - | 1863-07 | stated | A high point at the south end of the later national cemetery was where a signal station stood during the battle | #s4
28 | woodlots | Evergreen Cemetery grove cleared 1956 | - | ~1956 (clearing of the grove) | stated | The most significant loss in character occurred in 1956 from the clearing of the natural deciduous grove of trees in Evergreen Cemetery that bordered the south end of the national cemetery | #s10
21 | later | Baltimore Street gates and fence, Greek Revival gatehouse and outer stone wall of the national cemetery completed by May 1865 | - | !1865 | stated | By May 1865 the national cemetery's Baltimore Street entrance gates and fencing, stone Greek Revival gatehouse and outer stone boundary wall were completed | #s24
26 | later | Lafayette Square Fence along the Evergreen boundary installed 1934; Evergreen grove cleared 1956 | - | !1934-1956 | stated | A more distinct separation from Evergreen Cemetery came with the Lafayette Square Fence in 1934 and the clearing of the large grove at the south end of Evergreen Cemetery in 1956 | #s22
